NOTES = {
1: """[slide 1 of 25  |  1:00  |  cumulative 1:00 of 60:00]

Hi, I am Muntaser Syed. I am a researcher and PhD candidate here at Florida Tech, and I build software for a living. Thank you to Alexander and the ACM chapter for the invitation, and to Dr. Silaghi for the introduction. This talk is about hashrope, a data structure I maintain. It ships in Python, Rust, Java and TypeScript on one version line, so whatever stack you are learning or working in, there is a package for it. In the next hour I will show you what it is, why the arithmetic works, the measurements behind it, including the ones it loses, and where it earns its keep. There is a hands-on segment in the middle, so keep a phone or laptop reachable.""",

2: """[slide 2 of 25  |  1:00  |  cumulative 2:00 of 60:00]

The shape of the hour. First the problem: the workloads where plain strings and plain hashes stop working. Then the structure itself, built up from a rope you may already know. Then demos, in Python and TypeScript, and five minutes where you run it yourselves. Then the evidence: how four implementations are kept byte-identical, and the peer-reviewed measurements from ICTAI 2026, including the benchmarks hashrope loses; I will not hide those. Finally the uses, and a discussion at the end. Ask questions as they come to you; the discussion slot at the end is for the three arguments I actually want to have.""",

3: """[slide 3 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 4:30 of 60:00]

Start with the problem, because the title claims strings do not scale to everything, and that needs defending. A string is a fine structure for small, static text. Three pressures break it. First, size: when a sequence is megabytes to terabytes, logs, documents, genomes, model context, every copy and every hash is a full pass over n bytes, and n keeps growing. Second, change: editors, patches, streaming appends, tool results landing in an agent's context. The moment one byte changes, any whole-sequence hash you computed is stale, and recomputing it reads everything again. Third, sharing: a producer in Python, a consumer in a browser, a cache in Rust; every side needs the same identity for the same bytes and a cheap way to prove a copy is intact. What you want, all at once: fast edits, a fingerprint that is always current, and one comparison to verify a copy. hashrope is a rope whose every node carries a fingerprint. Edits stay logarithmic, and the root fingerprint is ready after every one of them. That is the whole talk; the rest is how, and whether it holds up.""",

4: """[slide 4 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 7:00 of 60:00]

Why is this not already standard? Because every fingerprinting tool you know makes you pick. Read the table by column. A rolling hash with prefix sums answers any substring query in constant time, which is excellent until one byte changes and the whole prefix table rebuilds in linear time. A Merkle tree or content-defined chunking survives edits, but it only knows block boundaries, so a substring hash at an arbitrary byte offset is not available, and concatenation means re-chunking. A plain rope gives logarithmic edits, splits and joins, but carries no fingerprint at all. And rehashing with SHA or xxHash is linear on every question. hashrope's row: logarithmic substring hash at any offset, logarithmic edits, splits and concats, the whole-sequence hash in O(1) at the root, repetition in O(log q) as a single node, and persistent versions. The one honest "no" in its row is cryptographic strength, and we will come back to that in the limits section. The reason the row is possible: fingerprints of parts combine into the fingerprint of the whole.""",

5: """[slide 5 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 9:30 of 60:00]

Build it from the rope, the part many of you already know. Take ten bytes: h a s h, space, r o p, e space. A rope stores the bytes in leaves and keeps a tree above them. Every internal node stores the length of the bytes below it and the weight, the leaf count. To find byte seven, compare against the left length at each level and descend; that path is logarithmic, so indexing, splitting and joining are logarithmic too. An insert at position i is a split at i, then a join of the left part, the new leaf, and the right part; only the path to the cut is rebuilt. And ropes are persistent by construction: an edit returns a new root and shares every unchanged subtree with the old one, so old versions stay valid. Ropes have carried text editors since Boehm, Atkinson and Plass in 1995. hashrope keeps all of that and adds exactly one field per node. Next slide.""",

6: """[slide 6 of 25  |  3:00  |  cumulative 12:30 of 60:00]

Here is the same tree with that one field. Every node now stores a polynomial hash of the bytes below it, and the numbers on the slide are real, from the explainer site. The leaf "hash" ends in 2bb9ca, " rop" ends in 8a57c7, the internal node above them ends in b7bcb1, and the root ends in d82b95. The rule is the whole trick: the hash of A followed by B equals the hash of A, shifted by the length of B, plus the hash of B. So an internal node computes its fingerprint once, from its two children, and never re-reads the bytes. Two consequences to hold on to. First, the root fingerprint equals the direct hash of the flat bytes, whatever shape the tree has; two ropes built with different chunking over the same bytes carry the same root, which is what makes cross-language and cross-system comparison meaningful. Second, verification is one comparison: equal roots, the copies match. Different roots, descend where the children disagree and you land on the first differing byte. One field read gives the fingerprint after any edit; there is no rehash pass, ever.""",

7: """[slide 7 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 15:00 of 60:00]

The arithmetic is three lines, and it is worth seeing because it is why four languages produce identical numbers. The base hash maps every byte to its value plus one, so a zero byte is not invisible and zero padding cannot collide with an empty sequence, and sums a polynomial in x. Concatenation shifts the left hash by x to the length of the right part and adds the right hash. Repetition of a pattern q times is the pattern's hash times a geometric sum, evaluated in O(log q) without a modular inverse. The modulus is 2^61 minus 1, a Mersenne prime, so reduction is a shift and an add, no division; that is what makes the arithmetic cheap and portable. The default base is x = 131, the same in all four languages. The profile, p and x, travels with every node, and mixing profiles in one fingerprint operation is rejected. Exact comparison, when we get to it, works across profiles because it compares bytes, not fingerprints.""",

8: """[slide 8 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 17:30 of 60:00]

Balance and persistence keep the logarithms honest. The tree uses strict weight balance, BB two-sevenths, maintained by an Adams-style balanced join. Concatenation goes through the join, and split and insert go through concatenation, so the height stays logarithmic after any sequence of operations; no edit history degrades the tree. Persistence is the picture: version one in gold. Edit one byte inside D, and the path from the root to D is rebuilt, a new D, a new B, a new root. A and C are shared; both versions point at them. Version one is untouched and still answers queries, and keeping both versions cost three new nodes, not a copy. Measured as a guard in the ICTAI experiments: a fork allocates at most ceil(log2 w) plus 3 nodes, and branch creation stayed flat at 26 to 37 microseconds from one thousand to one million tokens. Five branches of a one-million-token context cost about 9.2 kilobytes of new nodes, against 5.63 megabytes for a paged block table. That is the 613x figure; hold it for the research section.""",

9: """[slide 9 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 20:00 of 60:00]

Two more operations fall out of the same rule. Substring fingerprints: for any range, walk down to it, collect the maximal subtrees inside it, a logarithmic number, and fold their stored fingerprints with the concatenation rule. Only a partial leaf at either edge is hashed byte by byte, and nothing is allocated. That gives you a cache key for any slice, a prefix fingerprint for routing, or a window hash for dedup, all from maintained metadata. Then repeat nodes: a pattern repeated q times is one node holding the child and the count. The fingerprint of q copies is a closed-form geometric sum, so two to the sixtieth copies of a seven-byte pattern, about eight exabytes of represented length, has its length, its fingerprint, and a mid-sequence substring fingerprint computed in 130 microseconds, in two nodes of memory. Split, substring hash and exact comparison all work inside a repeat without expanding it. Tandem repeats in genomes, repeated boilerplate in documents, retried tool calls in agent logs: one node each.""",

10: """[slide 10 of 25  |  3:00  |  cumulative 23:00 of 60:00]

Now the code. This is Python, hashrope 0.3.4 from PyPI, zero dependencies. A hasher with the default profile. A one-megabyte rope built from four-kilobyte chunks, height ten. Then an edit in the middle: split at position five hundred thousand, join the left part, a four-byte leaf, and the right part. The root fingerprint is ready at once. On the right is what the notebook printed on one Colab core: the edit plus fingerprint took about 1.4 milliseconds in pure Python; rebuilding the byte string and rehashing all of it took about 220 milliseconds, a factor of 158. Then verification: flip one bit in a copy and the root comparison says False in one integer compare, and rope_lcp walks to the first differing byte, offset 123,456, in 0.16 milliseconds. Last block, keep it in mind for the limits section: the pair [0, 131, 42] and [1, 0, 42] has equal fingerprints and unequal bytes. Fingerprint equality is a candidate; rope_equal is the decision. If you are following along in the notebook, this is section by section what you will run in ten minutes.""",

11: """[slide 11 of 25  |  2:30  |  cumulative 25:30 of 60:00]

Same story in TypeScript, from the npm package. A text encoder because positions are bytes, the same default hasher, and hello world built from two leaves. Its fingerprint equals the direct hash of the string, 0x5f87bb7db23cc7b, and Python printed exactly the same number; that is the slide's whole point. The two-to-the-sixtieth repeat fingerprint and the substring in the middle also match Python digit for digit. Positions in JavaScript are bigints, which is why you see the n suffix in the split call. The one-megabyte edit plus fingerprint runs 0.85 milliseconds here against 119 milliseconds for the naive rehash. And this is not a slide-only claim: the same package runs in your browser on the explainer site, where every operation is checked against a naive rehash of the flat bytes, the verified tick in the corner. One number, four runtimes: Python in Colab, Node in Colab, Chrome on the site, and the Rust and Java test suites all produce the same fingerprint for the same bytes.""",

12: """[slide 12 of 25  |  5:00  |  cumulative 30:30 of 60:00]

Your turn, five minutes. Open hashrope.lovable.app on your phone or laptop, or the Colab notebook if you want the real packages. Three things to try. One: type something, edit one byte, and watch the root fingerprint change while the untouched subtrees keep theirs. Two: build the two-to-the-sixtieth repeat and read off a fingerprint for eight exabytes you do not have. Three: reproduce the collision pair from the Python demo, watch the fingerprints agree, then settle it with rope_equal and see the bytes disagree. Then compare your fingerprint for hello world with your neighbor's. It must be 0x5f87bb7db23cc7b in every language. If yours differs, that is the most interesting result in the room, so shout. I will keep the demo rope on screen and take questions while you run it. [Circulate; common snags: the npm positions are bigints; the playground verifies against a naive rehash, point at the tick.]""",

13: """[slide 13 of 25  |  2:00  |  cumulative 32:30 of 60:00]

The limits, on the box rather than in a footnote. One: not a cryptographic digest. It is a polynomial fingerprint; [0, 131] and [1, 0] collide at base 131, and crafted collisions exist for any fixed base. If an attacker is in your threat model, pair hashrope with SHA-256: SHA-256 for the integrity decision, hashrope for the cheap work. Two: fingerprint equality is a candidate match. When a decision depends on equality, decide with rope_equal, rope_lcp and the range variants; they compare bytes, skip shared subtrees, and never consult the fingerprint. Three: exact comparison has no universal log bound. Equal bytes stored independently get read; the shortcuts come from shared history and repetition, which is exactly where comparisons are cheap. Four: small, static data needs no rope. If it fits in a cache line and never changes, hash it once with xxHash and move on. And the standing rule for the project: the benchmarks hashrope loses are published next to the wins, in the repository.""",

14: """[slide 14 of 25  |  2:00  |  cumulative 34:30 of 60:00]

Four languages, one version line. Python on PyPI, 3.10 and up, pure Python. TypeScript and JavaScript on npm, Node 20.11 and up or any ES2022 browser, ESM and CommonJS builds. Rust on crates.io, no_std plus alloc with arena storage, and a bigint feature for Python-sized values. Java on Maven Central, Java 17 and up, BigInteger arithmetic. The table is the same five operations in four spellings: concat, split, substring hash, repeat, and the exact pair, equal and lcp. Learn one column and you know the others; the only real differences are the integer types, int, bigint, u64, BigInteger. All four core packages, plus hashrope-bio for Python and Rust, were published together at 0.3.4 on 24 October 2026. Same defaults, same fixtures, zero runtime dependencies, MIT licensed.""",
}

